    "id": "milazzo_tx_buddy",
    "title": {
      "en": "#7 Dr. Milazzo Revisited: Extending an Early Multi-Part WSPR Antenna Study with Same-Cycle Evidence"
    },
    "description": {
      "en": "[Technical article: Milazzo, C. F. / KP4MD (2011). Using the Weak Signal Propagation Reporter Network to Compare Antenna Performance](https://www.qsl.net/kp4md/wspr.htm)\n\nPublished in January 2011 using December 2010 observations, **Dr. Carol F. Milazzo’s work is an early WSPR antenna-comparison study**. Presented with **Brian Lloyd (WB6RQN)**, it brought together comparison through a common remote station, transmit-power correction, propagation predictions and reciprocal reception reports. The study deserves credit for demonstrating how WSPR can support practical investigations of antennas and complete stations, and forms part of the methodological lineage discussed in WSPRadar’s documentation.\n\nThe published analysis compared plotted SNR trends and did not specify the strict same-cycle, full-locator **Joint Spot** selection used here. Unequal operating schedules leave only a small synchronized subset on its selected VE6PDQ path. WSPRadar builds on this foundation by making exact pairing, reported-power normalization, the **Target-Active Gate**, and the separation of Joint and non-joint observations explicit and traceable.\n\nFor manual validation, we reconstructed evidence from the publication and reconciled **44 Figure 6 markers** and **47 individually resolved Figure 7 anchors** with historical reports at the declared common power scale. The Figure 7 anchors are a readable subset of the plotted evidence. These graphical matches are distinct from paired ΔSNR measurements: the reconstructed VE6PDQ path supplies only **five RX Joint Spots and one TX Joint Spot** under the current rules. This makes the case particularly useful for checking individual report identities, normalization and selection by hand, while the sparse paired evidence limits broader antenna conclusions.\n\n**Main walkthrough — Figure 6: VE6PDQ transmitting to KP4MD / WB6RQN.** The demo opens in **RX Benchmark**, comparing **Target receiver KP4MD** and **Reference receiver WB6RQN** on **40 m**, from **19 December 2010 12:00 to 20 December 2010 20:00 UTC**. The query includes transmitters within **5,000 km**; the reconstructed VE6PDQ path is the manual-validation example. Archived endpoint identities reconcile Figure 6 with reception **from VE6PDQ**, despite the original figure’s printed direction.\n\nStart with **VE6PDQ (DO34IR)**, already selected in **Station Insights**. Its reconstructed evidence contains **3 Joint Spots**, with ΔSNR values **+8, +22 and +7 dB** and median **+8 dB**. Then select **VE6PDQ (DO34)** for the other **2 Joint Spots**, **+17 and +7 dB**, with median **+12 dB**. Across these two reported identities, the five Figure 6 pairs have median **+8 dB**. Keep the full locators separate: a shared cycle does not create a Joint Spot when the reported transmitter locators differ.\n\nUse the **Figure 6 PDF** to follow the original reports in Panel A through the reconstruction in Panel B to the exact Joint Spots in Panel C. The faint dashed guides identify which same-cycle, full-locator report pairs survive into the paired view. The demo retains **3-hour bins** for the selected station; the two locator identities are inspected separately in the app.\n\nThe pair at **19 December 14:46 UTC**, with transmitter locator **DO34IR**, is easy to check by hand: KP4MD reports **+2 dB** and WB6RQN **−6 dB**, both for VE6PDQ at **5 W**. On WSPRadar’s common 1 W scale these become **−5 and −13 dB**, so **ΔSNR = +8 dB**. The common power adjustment cancels in this RX difference; positive ΔSNR means higher reported SNR at KP4MD.\n\nFor this RX comparison, a **Joint Spot** requires both receivers to decode the same transmitter and full locator in the same WSPR cycle. The **Target-Active Gate** asks whether KP4MD decoded at least one qualifying transmitter in that cycle. The PDF reconstructs the supplied VE6PDQ path; the full demo query also includes other transmitters that can establish Target activity, so its non-joint counts need not equal the bounded PDF reconstruction. These five pairs support a transparent manual check, while differences in receiving hardware, local noise and paths prevent interpreting the result as isolated antenna gain.\n\n[Review the reciprocal RX comparison in Figure 6 (PDF)](app/static/reference_figures/milazzo_publication_overlays_v1/WSPRadar_Demo_Milazzo_Figure6.pdf).\n\n**Reciprocal Figure 7 comparison — optional TX follow-up.** To revisit the transmit comparison below, switch **Analysis direction** to **TX**, keeping KP4MD as Target, WB6RQN as Reference, and the same dates, band and distance limit. The following counts and normalization example refer to that TX configuration; the installed demo starts with the RX Figure 6 walkthrough above.\n\nRevisits the **40 m KP4MD full-wave loop versus WB6RQN GAP Titan DX transmit comparison** for **19 December 2010 12:00–20 December 2010 20:00 UTC**, extending the analysis to receivers within **5,000 km**.\n\nIn **Segment Insight → Joint-Spot ΔSNR**, **45 Joint observations across 30 receivers and nine shared cycles** give mean **−3.96 dB** and median **−4 dB**, favoring WB6RQN after reported-power normalization.\n\nSelect **VE6PDQ (DO34ir)** in **Station Insights**: **1 Joint Spot, 56 Only KP4MD and 0 Only WB6RQN**. Eighteen additional WB6RQN reports fail the **Target-Active Gate** because no qualifying receiver reported KP4MD in those cycles. The gate retains a cycle only when KP4MD was reported by at least one receiver; it does not require both transmitters to be heard at every receiver.\n\nThe Joint pair at **20 December 00:22 UTC** illustrates normalization to 1 W: KP4MD’s **−13 dB at 5 W** becomes **−20 dB**, while WB6RQN’s **−15 dB at 2 W** becomes **−18 dB**. Thus **ΔSNR = −2 dB**.\n\nThis agrees with the article’s description of WB6RQN being **2–6 dB stronger in Figure 7**. Archived station identities indicate that its printed direction is reversed: Figure 7 mixes up TX and RX, describing transmissions **from VE6PDQ** that were actually received **at VE6PDQ**. \n\n[Review Figure 7 with reconstructed reports and Target-Active Gate markers (PDF)](app/static/reference_figures/milazzo_publication_overlays_v1/WSPRadar_Demo_Milazzo_Figure7.pdf). \n\n KP4MD operated continuously on the frequency while WB6RQN frequency-hopped, so non-joint counts reflect operating schedules as well as reception and cannot isolate antenna performance. Only **Joint Spots** enter the ΔSNR comparison: both transmitters must be decoded at the same receiving station and locator in the same WSPR cycle. This narrower synchronized subset, with reported-power normalization, compares relative SNR on the sampled paths; it does not establish direction-independent antenna gain."
    }
  },
